	•	Section 2 Comments/Quotes: The primary CTOT-20 analytic cohort contained 181 recipients contributing 806 pre-CLAD BAL-cell-pellet samples that passed RNA quality control. Mean age was 55.2 years and 80 of 181 recipients (44.2%) were female. Race and ethnicity, native lung-disease category, transplant type, Lung Allocation Score, HLA mismatch, induction therapy, and grade 3 primary graft dysfunction were reported. The independent UCLA cohort contained 219 recipients, although external diagnostic validation was performed in 78 samples meeting the clinically significant ACR or stable-control phenotype definitions.

Section 3: Intervention & AI Methods
	•	Primary ML Component: Yes
	•	Study Design: Prospective Cohort
	•	AI Model Architecture: Random Forest
	•	Algorithm Name: 31-gene bronchoalveolar-lavage cell-pellet genomic ACR Random Forest classifier
	•	Input Variables (Data Modality): Multi-omics/Genetics
	•	Comparator / Standard of Care: Human expert/Clinician
	•	Validation Method: External Validation (Geographic/Different Hospital)
	•	Explainability / Interpretability Used: Yes (e.g., SHAP, LIME)
	•	Feature Selection Method: Automated (e.g., LASSO, Stepwise)
	•	Hyperparameter Tuning Reported: Yes
	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: RNA-seq-derived gene-expression features were used to classify clinically significant ACR versus stable controls defined by bronchoscopy, microbiology, donor-specific antibodies, and pathologist-assigned transbronchial-biopsy grades. Logistic regression, Elastic-Net, and Random Forest were evaluated; Random Forest was selected. Ten-fold cross-validation tuned mtry from 1–15 and ntree from 100–5000. Boruta automated all-relevant feature selection reduced 62 differentially expressed genes to a locked 31-gene model. Interpretability was provided through individual gene-importance rankings and biologic pathway analysis. Validation included a held-out internal test set and an independent geographically separate UCLA cohort.

Section 4: AI Quality & Reproducibility (CONVINCE Standards)
	•	Missing Data Handling: Not Reported
	•	Class Imbalance Addressed: No
	•	Code Availability: Not Reported
	•	Data Preprocessing / Normalization Described: Yes
	•	Training Size (N): 150
	•	Test Size (N): 78
	•	Section 4 Comments/Quotes: RNA quantity and integrity thresholds were prespecified; 185 of 991 pre-CLAD samples failed quality control, predominantly because of insufficient RNA quantity. Sequencing preprocessing included fastp read cleaning, quality metrics, STAR alignment, FeatureCounts quantification, RPKM calculation, low-expression filtering, and LIMMA-voom differential-expression analysis. No missing-predictor imputation was described. Clinically significant ACR was substantially less frequent than stable control in the model-development sample, but no class weighting, over-sampling, under-sampling, or other imbalance method was reported. The development split contained 150 training and 38 internal-test samples; the independent external validation analysis contained 78 samples. Gene-expression and clinical data were deposited in Mendeley Data, but source code or trained model weights were not reported.

