	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: Performance was extracted from the independent UCLA validation cohort for distinguishing clinically significant ACR from stable controls. The 31-gene Random Forest classifier achieved AUC 0.90 (95% CI 0.83–0.96), accuracy 82.1%, sensitivity 73.3%, and specificity 87.5%. PPV, NPV, and F1-score were not reported for the external cohort. The internal held-out test set contained 38 samples and achieved AUC 0.72 (95% CI 0.53–0.88). Formal probability calibration and decision-curve analysis were not reported. As a secondary prognostic finding, late genomic ACR was associated with subsequent CLAD risk.

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Although CTOT-20 was prospective and multicenter, the analysis used BAL samples from only 200 consecutively transplanted participants, excluded samples failing RNA quality control, and developed the diagnostic model using only 37 clinically significant ACR and 151 highly selected stable-control samples. This case-versus-stable-control spectrum does not represent the full range of routine post-transplant bronchoscopy findings.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: BAL-cell-pellet gene expression was measured using a standardized laboratory workflow with prespecified RNA quantity and integrity thresholds, read-quality filtering, reference-genome alignment, and quantitative expression processing. Predictor measurement was performed on the same BAL specimen obtained at the diagnostic assessment and did not depend on subsequent clinical outcomes.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The reference diagnosis depended primarily on pathologist-assigned transbronchial-biopsy A-grade. The authors note that TBB is limited by sampling error, inadequate or suboptimal specimens, and poor interobserver agreement between expert pathologists. These limitations create substantial risk of reference-standard misclassification.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Differential-expression screening appears to have identified the 62 candidate genes using the full clinically significant ACR and stable-control dataset before the 80:20 model split, creating potential information leakage. Repeated samples were explicitly treated as independent observations, and random sample-level splitting could place samples from the same recipient in both training and test sets. Additional concerns include only 37 positive samples, no reported class-imbalance handling, and no calibration analysis, despite hyperparameter tuning, Boruta selection, internal testing, and external validation.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly evaluates a machine-learning genomic classifier for acute cellular rejection in post-transplant lung recipients and compares it with the current biopsy-based diagnostic standard. It is directly applicable to AI and machine-learning applications in thoracic organ transplantation.

